<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parantaka I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/907">907</a>–<a href="/years/955">955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Parantaka Chola I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parantaka I was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a> who ruled from 907 to 955. He conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> kingdom around 920, taking the title Maduraikonda, captor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, and campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Inscriptions of his reign at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttaramerur">Uttaramerur</a> describe in detail how village assemblies were elected. In 949 the Rashtrakuta king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> at Takkolam, and the kingdom lost its northern lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parantaka-i.jpg" alt="Parantaka I" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parantaka_territories.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Parantaka was the elder son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aditya-i">Aditya I</a>, born to a Chera wife; his younger half-brother, Kannara Deva, was born to a Rashtrakuta wife. When Aditya died, the Rashtrakuta king Krishna II tried to place his grandson Kannara Deva on the Chola throne. Parantaka became king instead in 907, and Krishna II invaded. At the Battle of Vallala in 911, where Parantaka and Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaditya">Rajaditya</a> led the Chola side against Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-iii">Indra III</a>, the Rashtrakutas lost many men and were driven out of Chola territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Parantaka took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> early in his reign and called himself Maturai Koṇṭāṉ, Capturer of Madurai. The Pandyan ruler Maravarman Rajasinha II sought help from Kassapa V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, but the combined army was defeated at Vellur, and the Pandyan king fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> with his royal insignia. When the Sinhalese kings refused to return them, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> army invaded the island around 946–947, sacked Anuradhapura and took the north, but Udaya IV hid the insignia in the Rohana hills; they were recovered only under Parantaka's grandson Rajendra I.</p><p>He also subdued the Bana and Vaidumba chiefs allied with the Rashtrakutas, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> inscription at Bairakur suggests a clash with the Nolamba ruler Iriva Nolamba Diliparaja around 936. In 949 the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> at Takkolam, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaditya">Rajaditya</a> was killed by an arrow shot by Butuga, a Ganga chief allied with the Rashtrakutas. Inscriptions from South Arcot of 952–954 acknowledge neither Chola nor Rashtrakuta rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>For all his wars, Parantaka laid down rules for the conduct of village assemblies, dug canals across the country according to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate inscriptions, and gave spoils of war to temple charities. A devout follower of Siva, he is reported to have covered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chidambaram">Chidambaram</a> temple with a golden roof, perhaps restoring an older one that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirumangai-alvar">Tirumangai Alvar</a> had already described.</p><p>At least eleven of his wives appear in inscriptions, among them a Chera princess, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaditya">Rajaditya</a>, whose marriage strengthened his alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. His other sons included Arikulakesari, Gandaraditya and Uttamasili. Parantaka died in 955 and was succeeded by his second son, Gandaraditya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parantaka I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor Aditya I defeated the Pallava and Pandyan dynasties early in the 10th century, capturing Tondaimandalam and Kongu Nadu, and Aditya's son <b>Parantaka I</b> defeated the Rashtrakuta dynasty in the battle of Vallala and the Pandyas again.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 920 <b>Parantaka I</b> of the Chola Empire defeated Rajasimha III, who fled to Sri Lanka.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-pandyan-empire">First Pandyan Empire</a></li></ul></section>
